From: Silicon Bakery <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: apple: t6020-j414s: use the PMP, opt-in

Define APPLE_USE_PMP for the 14" M2 Pro. The pmp alias it adds is what
m1n1 (dt_set_pmp) looks for: it copies the board ID, DRAM vendor and the
ADT's /arm-io/pmp/iop-pmp-nub tunables into the node and enables it, so
apple_pmp starts the Power Management Processor. DCP, the external DCPs,
AVD and the ISP's DART then take their power domains from the PMP report
entries, so the coprocessor knows when they are on.

t602x leaves pmp_report_isp_sys disabled and no t602x board enables it,
unlike the M1 Pro/Max and M2 Air boards; with the define alone the ISP's
DART would wait for a power domain that never appears and the camera
would not probe. Enable it here.

Asahi measured about 0.5 W less at idle on a 14" M1 Pro (progress
report 7.0) and calls the driver unvalidated on most machines. t602x has
no apple,pio-ranges for the PMP (t600x does; t8112 does not either, and
the ADT has none), so the driver answers its IOVA table request empty.
Not booted on an M2 Pro.

---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6020-j414s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6020-j414s.dts
@@ -9,6 +9,9 @@
 
 /dts-v1/;
 
+/* m1n1 starts the PMP; DCP, AVD and the ISP report through it */
+#define APPLE_USE_PMP
+
 #include "t6020.dtsi"
 #include "t602x-j414-j416.dtsi"
 
@@ -45,3 +48,8 @@
 &mtp_mt {
 	firmware-name = "apple/tpmtfw-j414s.bin";
 };
+
+/* t602x leaves this entry disabled; the ISP's DART needs it with PMP */
+&pmp_report_isp_sys {
+	status = "okay";
+};
